## HoneyHive explains how human trace reviews become evaluation checks

Source: HoneyHive — https://www.honeyhive.ai/learn/human-evaluation-llm
Published: 2026-11-08
Channel: news

HoneyHive is an agent observability and evaluation platform that records execution traces and supports human review. A trace captures the steps of an application run. Its guide to human evaluation addresses a practical problem: teams need clear examples of what went wrong before they can decide what an automated evaluator should check. The guide recommends people for subjective qualities and domain-specific judgments that automation cannot yet assess reliably.

First, sample diverse real traces and let a domain expert write open notes about failures. Group those notes into recurring failure types before writing a rubric, so the criteria do not hide unexpected problems. Then turn the failure types into pass-or-fail questions. When several reviewers participate, have them label the same examples independently, measure agreement and revise unclear criteria.

Finally, use the labeled traces to test automated checks and model-based judges. In HoneyHive, teams create human evaluator fields, select trace events and add them to an annotation queue. Queue automation can also add matching events continuously. The guide describes a review workflow; it does not demonstrate that any particular automated judge will match a team's experts.

Why it matters: For teams defining evaluation criteria: review real failures before fixing the rubric, then check automated judgments against expert labels.

